WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.383
Actions on objectives describes the point where the adversary attempts to achieve the intended impact.

2
00:00:06.533 --> 00:00:13.341
The objective may be data theft, fraud, disruption, sabotage, account abuse, ransomware impact, business email compromise,

3
00:00:13.491 --> 00:00:16.896
operational interference, or something specific to the organization.

4
00:00:17.046 --> 00:00:21.727
Defenders use this stage to connect technical evidence to business risk.

5
00:00:21.877 --> 00:00:24.077
What data was touched?

6
00:00:24.227 --> 00:00:26.427
Which privileged actions occurred?

7
00:00:26.577 --> 00:00:28.777
Which accounts approved changes?

8
00:00:28.927 --> 00:00:31.127
Did backups remain usable?

9
00:00:31.277 --> 00:00:33.477
Were transactions altered?

10
00:00:33.627 --> 00:00:36.180
Did a business process stop working?

11
00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:44.415
Monitoring data access, identity activity, privileged actions, payment or workflow changes, and unusual export behavior can help identify impact.

12
00:00:44.565 --> 00:00:47.118
Recovery planning matters here as well.

13
00:00:47.268 --> 00:00:55.779
Backups, continuity procedures, transaction review, and communication plans reduce the chance that a late-stage incident becomes a prolonged business crisis.

14
00:00:55.929 --> 00:00:58.907
This stage should also feed lessons learned.

15
00:00:59.057 --> 00:01:05.866
If the team only restores service and moves on, it misses the chance to strengthen controls.

16
00:01:06.016 --> 00:01:13.676
Good analysis of actions on objectives explains what outcome was attempted or achieved, what evidence supports that conclusion,

17
00:01:13.826 --> 00:01:18.081
what business impact remains, and what control improvements should follow.

18
00:01:18.231 --> 00:01:21.635
Late-stage analysis should be tied to business language.

19
00:01:21.785 --> 00:01:28.594
A leader may not need every technical detail, but they do need to understand whether data,

20
00:01:28.744 --> 00:01:32.573
money, availability, safety, trust, or regulatory obligations were affected.

21
00:01:32.723 --> 00:01:36.128
That translation helps response priorities match organizational risk.
